## Proof

Let $r$ be a root of the quadratic $x^2 - kx - 1 = 0$ with $|r| \le 1$. Such a root exists: the discriminant is $k^2+4>0$, giving two real roots $r_1,r_2$ with $r_1 r_2 = -1$, hence $|r_1|\,|r_2|=1$; at least one of them satisfies $|r|\le 1$. Note also $r\ne 0$.

Set $b_i := a_i - k\,a_{i+1} - a_{i+2}$ for $i=0,1,\dots,n-2$. We compute the weighted sum $\sum_{i=0}^{n-2} r^i b_i$ by collecting the coefficient of each $a_j$:

$$\sum_{i=0}^{n-2} r^i b_i = \sum_{j=0}^{n-2} r^j a_j \;-\; k\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} r^{j-1} a_j \;-\; \sum_{j=2}^{n} r^{j-2} a_j.$$

The coefficient of $a_j$ is $r^j - k\,r^{j-1} - r^{j-2} = r^{j-2}(r^2-kr-1)$ for $2\le j\le n-2$, which equals $0$ since $r^2-kr-1=0$. The coefficient of $a_0$ is $r^0=1$. The coefficient of $a_n$ is $-r^{n-2}$. The terms involving $a_1$ and $a_{n-1}$ vanish because $a_1=a_{n-1}=0$. Therefore

$$\sum_{i=0}^{n-2} r^i b_i \;=\; a_0 \;-\; r^{n-2}\,a_n,$$

or equivalently,

$$a_0 \;=\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-2} r^i\, b_i \;+\; r^{n-2}\, a_n.$$

Taking absolute values and applying the triangle inequality:

$$|a_0| \;\le\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-2} |r|^i\,|b_i| \;+\; |r|^{n-2}\,|a_n|.$$

Since $|r|\le 1$, we have $|r|^i\le 1$ for every $i\ge 0$ and $|r|^{n-2}\le 1$ (as $n>1$). Hence

$$|a_0| \;\le\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-2}|b_i| \;+\; |a_n|.$$

Subtracting $|a_n|$ from both sides yields

$$|a_0|-|a_n| \;\le\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-2}|b_i| \;=\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-2}\bigl|a_i - k\,a_{i+1} - a_{i+2}\bigr|,$$

which is the desired inequality. $\blacksquare$

